   "overview": "St. George Fire Protection District – Assistant Chief Evaluation & Testing Framework\n\nPurpose\n\nTo evaluate the performance and readiness of Assistant Chiefs (Shift Commanders) to function as senior operational leaders overseeing all SGFD field operations during their assigned shift, ensuring alignment with department policy, NFPA standards, and Civil Service expectations.\n\nAssistant Chiefs serve as the Fire Chief’s on-duty representative, responsible for tactical command, operational readiness, personnel management, and interdivisional coordination during emergencies.\n\nRole Context\n\nLevel | Focus | Typical Scope\nBattalion Chief | Tactical / District command | 3–4 stations, single-alarm incidents\n\nAssistant Chief (Shift Commander) | Strategic / Multi-district command | All districts, all battalions, Type 3+ incidents\n\nDeputy Chief | Executive / Administrative command | Department-wide policy, programs, and divisions\n\nEvaluation Domains (Shift Commander Level)\n\nDomain\n1. Strategic Incident Command – Manage large-scale or multiple concurrent incidents as the on-duty chief officer; ensure tactical accountability, safety, and coordination.\n\n2️. Shift-Wide Leadership & Readiness – Oversee daily staffing, station coverage, apparatus readiness, and shift briefings.\n\n3. Policy Execution & Compliance – Enforce SGFD SOPs, policies, and operational directives; coordinate with Training and Administration on deficiencies.\n\n4. Personnel Management & Mentorship – Supervise Battalion Chiefs and company officers; handle corrective action, evaluations, and performance improvement.\n\n5. Critical Communication & Liaison – Maintain inter-agency and public communication (law enforcement, EMS, utilities, OEM, etc.) during events.\n\n6. Professionalism & Command Presence – Demonstrate ethical conduct, composure, and effective decision-making as the highest-ranking officer on duty.\n\nProposed Module Structure (1-Year Working Test Period)\n\nModule | Focus | Description | Deliverable\nA | Multi-Incident Command | Command multiple simultaneous incidents (simulated or live) | Incident Action Plan (IAP) + ICS Forms\n\nB | Shift Operations & Staffing Control | Manage complex staffing, recalls, and apparatus coverage | Staffing Log & Shift Summary Report\n\nC | Policy & Operational Compliance | Audit company operations and enforce policy alignment | Operational Audit Memo & Corrective Plan\n\nD | Personnel & Leadership Management | Evaluate Battalion Chiefs and handle performance issues | Counseling or Mentorship Report\n\nE | Interagency Coordination & Communications | Oversee coordination with BRPD, EMS, OEM during events | Unified Command Report\n\nF | Professionalism & Command Readiness | Evaluate ethics, conduct, and decision-making under pressure | Reflective Essay & Evaluator Observation\n\nEvaluation Framework\n\nEach module is:\n\n-   Conducted on duty (no overtime).\n\n-   Evaluated by Deputy Chief of Operations or Fire Chief.\n\n-   Scored 0–25 points using the same five-criteria rubric:\n\n1.  Operational Decision-Making\n\n2.  Policy Compliance\n\n3.  Leadership & Communication\n\n4.  Documentation & Reporting\n\n5.  Professionalism & Command Presence\n\nDocumentation Requirements\n\nIncludes:\n\n-   Completed deliverables (reports, memos, IAPs)\n\n-   Evaluation rubric and scoring sheet\n\n-   Working Test Period Summary Form\n\nCivil Service Integration\n\n-   Conducted during the 1-year Working Test Period.\n\n-   Each quarter, the Assistant Chief completes two modules.\n\n-   Satisfactory completion of all six modules = recommendation for permanent appointment.",
