     "guide": "Module A – Strategic Oversight & District Continuity (Senior Advisor Role)\n\nPurpose\n\nTo evaluate the Assistant Chief’s ability to perform as the senior advisor and shift commander during a major incident, maintaining district-wide situational awareness, coordinating resources, supporting the Incident Commander (IC) with strategic input, and ensuring uninterrupted service coverage throughout SGFD.\n\nScenario Overview\n\nScenario Title:\n\n“District-Wide Readiness During a Second-Alarm Industrial Fire”\n\nScenario Conditions:\n\nAt 18:45 hours, a fire is reported at Riverbend Logistics Park (District 6). Battalion 6 arrives and establishes command. The fire quickly escalates, prompting a 2nd alarm.\nAs the on-duty Assistant Chief, you respond in your role as Senior Advisor / Shift Commander.\n\nDuring this incident:\n\n-   Six companies are committed on scene.\n\n-   Two simultaneous EMS calls and one automatic alarm occur elsewhere in the district.\n\n-   A thunderstorm is approaching within the hour.\n\n-   Baton Rouge FD offers a single mutual-aid engine (15-minute ETA).\n\n-   Dispatch requests guidance on backfill and move-ups.\n\nYou do not assume tactical command.\n\nYour job is to:\n\n-   Support the IC’s operational plan with strategic guidance.\n\n-   Maintain system-level coverage and readiness across SGFD.\n\n-   Anticipate extended duration impacts and resource fatigue.\n\n-   Communicate major system changes to command/communications.\n\n-   Document all coverage and strategic decisions for after-action review.\n\nOfficer Tasks / Decision Points\n\n1.  Advisor Support to the Incident Commander\n\n- | Provide a strategic assessment of resource needs, weather, and duration.\n\n- | Recommend escalation, staging, or relief planning as appropriate.\n\n- | Ensure span-of-control integrity and that accountability, rehab, and safety functions are staffed.\n\n- | Maintain clear boundaries—advise without overtaking command.\n\n2.  District Continuity & Resource Management\n\n- | Evaluate system coverage via CAD/FireWorks.\n\n- | Direct Battalion Chiefs to reposition companies for coverage.\n\n- | Coordinate mutual-aid assignments per Policy 410.00 – Mutual Aid & Coverage Assignments.\n\n- | Confirm EMS and Haz-Mat readiness for secondary events.\n\n3.  Strategic Communication & Coordination\n\n- | Maintain real-time communication with dispatch and the Fire Chief/Deputy Chief.\n\n- | Ensure all unit moves and mutual-aid requests are logged.\n\n- | Advise the PIO of district impact if public information releases are pending.\n\n4.  Documentation & Review\n\n- | Produce a District Continuity Log (unit status before/after the incident).\n\n- | Complete a Strategic Advisor Summary Memo outlining:\n\n- | Coverage actions taken.\n\n- | Inter-agency coordination.\n\n- | Recommendations for improving readiness.\n\n🔹 Applicable SGFD Policies\n\nPolicy | Title | Relevance\n104.00 | Chain of Command & Officer Responsibilities | Defines Assistant Chief’s role as shift commander and senior advisor.\n\n401.00 | Incident Command General Procedures | Outlines advisory and command-support roles at major incidents.\n\n404.00 | Multi-Company & Alarm Assignments | Establishes multi-alarm escalation and deployment structure.\n\n410.00 | Mutual Aid & Coverage Assignments | Governs back-fill and inter-district coverage procedures.\n\n412.00 | Operational Communications | Sets standards for radio discipline and dispatch coordination.\n\n900.00 | Policy Review & Records Management | Requires written post-incident documentation.\n\nEvaluator Observations\n\nPerformance Element | Max Pts (0–5) | Comments\nEffectiveness as Strategic Advisor | 5\n\nMaintenance of District Coverage | 5\n\nCommunication & Coordination with Dispatch/IC | 5\n\nDocumentation Quality (Log & Memo) | 5\n\nProfessionalism & Command Presence | 5\n\nTotal (25 pts)\n\nEvaluator Discussion Prompts\n\n-   How did you balance supporting the IC without overstepping command?\n\n-   What were your coverage priorities once multiple units were committed?\n\n-   How did you decide when to request or release mutual aid?\n\n-   What information did you provide the Fire Chief during the event?\n\n-   How would you improve shift-wide readiness next time?\n\nExpected Assistant Chief Behaviors (High-Scoring Examples)\n\n-   Maintains composure and system-level awareness under stress.\n\n-   Provides timely, actionable advice to the IC.\n\n-   Manages unit coverage with minimal service disruption.\n\n-   Communicates effectively with BCs, dispatch, and executive staff.\n\n-   Documents coverage actions clearly and completely.\n\n-   Reinforces SGFD policies without encroaching on the IC’s authority.\n\nDeliverables (Candidate)\n\nSubmit within 48 hours of the incident or simulation:\n\n1.  District Continuity Log – showing all unit moves and coverage outcomes.\n\n2.  Strategic Advisor Summary Memo (1–2 pages).\n\n3.  Optional: CAD map screenshot or resource movement chart.\n\nUpload to:\nAbsorb\n\nTraining Objective Reference\n\n-   NFPA 1021 (2020 Ed.) 6.2.1–6.2.3 – Serve in a command-support role at major incidents.\n\n-   NFPA 1561 (2020 Ed.) – Incident Management System; Senior Advisor Functions.\n\n-   SGFD Policy Manual: 104.00, 401.00, 404.00, 410.00, 412.00, 900.00.\n\n-   ISO/PIAL Credit: Command Support & Continuity Documentation.\n\nOutcome\n\nSuccessful completion demonstrates that the Assistant Chief:\n\n-   Provides sound strategic guidance without disrupting the command chain.\n\n-   Maintains operational readiness district-wide.\n\n-   Communicates and documents effectively with all stakeholders.\n\n-   Exemplifies SGFD’s expectations for shift-level executive command readiness."
