     "guide": "Module B – Shift Operations & Staffing Control (Expanded Real-World Scenario)\n\nPurpose\n\nTo evaluate the Assistant Chief’s ability to maintain operational readiness while simultaneously managing unexpected events, policy-driven demands, and personnel issues across multiple battalions—without compromising service coverage or compliance.\n\nScenario Overview\n\nScenario Title:\n\n“One Problem After Another”\n\nScenario Conditions (Current Time 05: 45 hours):\n\nYou are the on-duty Assistant Chief (Shift Commander).\nYesterday’s schedule and maintenance plans were finalized, but since midnight, the following have unfolded:\n\n1. Unscheduled Sick Call – Station 63\n\n-   A firefighter reports flu-like symptoms at 04: 30 hours.\n\n-   This reduces Engine 633 below minimum staffing.\n\n-   All available detail firefighters are already assigned elsewhere.\n\nDecision Point: Recall a firefighter, merge with Ladder 638 for the day, or authorize overtime.\n\n2. Engine 613 Mechanical Failure\n\n-   Returning from a 02: 10 medical call, Engine 643 threw a serpentine belt and is being towed to Maintenance.\n\n-   Maintenance estimates a 10-hour repair window but warns parts availability is uncertain.\n\nDecision Point: Assign temporary coverage (move-up or cross-staff Aerial 638) and communicate contingency plan to dispatch.\n\n3. Training Deficiency\n\n-   Training Division reports Station 62 is two weeks behind on drill hours and requests them for a live-fire evolution today (0900–1300).\n\n-   Weather later may cancel tomorrow’s makeup window.\n\nDecision Point: Approve, shorten, or reschedule the drill given coverage strain.\n\n4. Severe Weather Forecast\n\n-   National Weather Service updates: damaging winds and lightning expected by 1500 hours.\n\n-   You must plan early storm readiness and ensure reserve generators are tested district-wide.\n\nDecision Point: Initiate pre-storm checklist and direct BCs to confirm station preparedness.\n\n5. Station 61 Air-Conditioning Outage\n\n-   HVAC contractor can’t respond until after noon.\n\n-   Crews report inside temps exceeding 80°F and bunkroom humidity.\n\nDecision Point: Relocate personnel for rest periods or authorize temporary fans from logistics.\n\n6. Disciplinary Carry-Over\n\n-   A probationary firefighter from Station 65 has an unresolved counseling form for repeated tardiness.\n\n-   The captain asks whether to issue written documentation today or wait until his evaluation week.\n\nDecision Point: Advise on timing and documentation route per Policy 205 (Employee Performance).\n\n7. Hydrant System Notification\n\n-   City Utilities emails at 06: 10 hours — hydrant flow testing near Airline & Barringer from 10:00–14:00 hours.\n\n-   This may reduce pressure in the 60/63 area during peak call hours.\n\nDecision Point: Notify BCs and Prevention, adjust response matrix if needed.\n\n8. Public Information Request\n\n-   Local news outlet left voicemail asking about last night’s apartment fire video circulating online.\n\n-   The Fire Chief is off duty; the Deputy Chief of Operations requests you coordinate a consistent statement with the PIO.\n\nDecision Point: Determine what information can be released and route communication through proper channels.\n\n9. Mutual-Aid Inquiry\n\n-   BRFD asks if SGFD can send a Squad or Ladder to stand by for their Haz-Mat drill (1300–1600).\n\n-   Participation would provide training credit but may leave SGFD thin if weather worsens.\n\nDecision Point: Approve or decline; justify either way.\n\n10. Employee Injury Report\n\n-   Captain at Station 62 files minor injury paperwork for a firefighter who strained a shoulder yesterday but remained on duty.\n\n-   Worker’s Comp packet incomplete.\n\nDecision Point: Ensure documentation accuracy and notify Admin before end of shift.\n\nOfficer Tasks / Decision Flow\n\n1.  Triage and Prioritize – Sort all ten issues by immediacy and potential service impact.\n\n2.  Delegate Effectively – Assign BCs tasks with clear expectations.\n\n3.  Maintain Coverage – Balance apparatus moves and staffing fills under Policies 302 & 303.\n\n4.  Coordinate Externally – Liaise with Mainenance, City Utilities, BRFD, and PIO.\n\n5.  Document Completely – Produce a unified Shift Summary Report noting actions, policy references, and outcomes.\n\nEvaluator Observations\n\nPerformance Element | Max Pts (0–5) | Comments\nPrioritization of Concurrent Issues | 5\n\nPolicy Application & Decision Logic | 5\n\nDelegation & Communication with BCs / External Agencies | 5\n\nDocumentation & Transparency | 5\n\nProfessional Composure & Command Presence | 5\n\nTotal (25 pts)\n\nEvaluator Discussion Prompts\n\n-   Which issue carried the highest operational risk?\n\n-   How did you keep coverage intact while addressing lower-priority problems?\n\n-   Which decisions required consultation with the Deputy Chief or PIO?\n\n-   How would you improve shift-planning to prevent future cascade failures?\n\nDeliverables (Candidate)\n\n1.  Shift Staffing Summary Report (all ten issues, outcomes, times).\n\n2.  Recall / Coverage Log and Fleet Status Update.\n\n3.  Email Summary to Deputy Chief of Operations (CC Fire Chief and Admin).\n\n4.  Any Follow-Up Forms (e.g., Injury Report verification or Counseling Form routing).\n\nUpload to:\nAbsorb\n\nOutcome\n\nCompletion demonstrates the Assistant Chief can:\n\n-   Manage 10 + simultaneous issues without losing district readiness.\n\n-   Apply SGFD policy and Civil Service rules consistently.\n\n-   Communicate clearly up and down the chain of command.\n\n-   Model executive-level calm and competence under realistic workload."
