     "guide": "Module C – Battalion Oversight & Policy-Compliance Quality Control\n\n(Structured for SG6, SG7, and coordination with SG82)\n\nPurpose\n\nTo evaluate the Assistant Chief’s ability to ensure that each Battalion Chief under their command (SG6 and SG7) is properly conducting policy, documentation, and readiness quality control on their Captains and companies—while also confirming coordination standards are maintained with Special Operations (SG82).\nThis module focuses on oversight of the oversight process, not re-auditing company work.\n\nScenario Overview\n\nScenario Title:\n\n“Auditing the Auditors”\n\nScenario Conditions:\n\nYou are the Assistant Chief (Shift Commander) on duty overseeing SG6 and SG7, with coordination responsibility for SG82.\n\nA recent Deputy Chief Operations meeting identified uneven compliance among the battalions regarding documentation accuracy, timeliness of NERIS closures, and follow-through on training and PPE inspections.\n\nYou are directed to perform a Battalion Quality-Control Review across your shift to determine:\n\n-   How well each Battalion Chief is reviewing and correcting their Captains’ work.\n\n-   Whether the QC process itself aligns with SGFD policy and ISO/PIAL standards.\n\n-   If Special Ops documentation (SG82) integrates correctly with suppression battalions for shared incidents.\n\nKnown Conditions (Shift Start – 05:45 hours)\n\n# | Observation | What It Suggests\n1 | SG6 – Several daily logs signed by BC without driver signoffs; two incident reports missing incident numbers. | QC review may be superficial or rushed.\n\n2 | SG7 – NERIS reports consistently closed, but PPE and apparatus checklists lack BC verification signatures. | Process thoroughness inconsistent.\n\n3 | SG82 (Special Ops) – Submitted haz-mat training and deployment data directly to Training Division, bypassing BC QC chain. | Coordination gap between divisions.\n\n4 | Both Battalions – Driver training hours vary widely between stations; one BC using outdated spreadsheet instead of FireWorks. | Out-of-policy documentation practice.\n\n5 | Battalion 1 & 2 trend – Average NERIS closure time: SG6 = 36 hrs; SG7 = 18 hrs. | Possible imbalance in enforcement standards.\n\nOfficer Tasks / Decision Points\n\n1.  Audit Each Battalion’s QC Process\n\n- | Review BCs’ monthly review logs, signoffs, and training records.\n\n- | Identify whether the BC merely initials or actually verifies completeness.\n\n2.  Compare Standards Between Battalions\n\n- | Quantify turnaround times, error rates, and completeness.\n\n- | Determine whether differences are justified by workload or indicative of inconsistency.\n\n3.  Integrate Special Ops (SG82)\n\n- | Confirm that Special Ops documentation flows properly through suppression battalion reporting channels.\n\n- | If not, issue coordination guidance under Policy 410 – Mutual Aid & Inter-Division Operations.\n\n4.  Coach and Correct\n\n- | Meet with SG6 and SG7 BCs individually to review findings.\n\n- | Provide written feedback identifying three strengths and three areas for improvement.\n\n- | Reinforce Policies 301, 302, 900, and 902 to standardize processes.\n\n5.  Report Upward\n\n- | Compile a Battalion QC Compliance Summary for the Deputy Chief of Operations outline:\n\n- | Findings by battalion/division\n\n- | Corrective actions taken\n\n- | Follow-up deadlines and responsible officers\n\nApplicable SGFD Policies\n\nPolicy | Title | Relevance\n104.00 | Chain of Command & Officer Responsibilities | Defines AC oversight of BC performance.\n\n205.00 | Employee Performance Expectations | Guides coaching and accountability.\n\n301.00 | Training Records & Documentation | Ensures accuracy of drill and driver records.\n\n302.00 | Daily Staffing and Reports | Requires verified logs and checklists.\n\n410.00 | Mutual Aid & Inter-Division Operations | Covers Suppression/Special Ops coordination.\n\n900.00 | Records Management & Retention | Controls documentation corrections.\n\n902.00 | Information Management & Data Integrity | Governs NERIS accuracy and closure standards.\n\nEvaluator Observations\n\nPerformance Element | Max Pts (0–5)\nThoroughness of Battalion QC Review | 5\n\nAccuracy of Policy Application | 5\n\nCoaching and Feedback to BCs | 5\n\nIntegration with SG82 Documentation Flow | 5\n\nProfessionalism & Follow-Through | 5\n\nTotal (25 pts)\n\nEvaluator Discussion Prompts\n\n-   How did you differentiate BC oversight failures from company-level errors?\n\n-   What metrics did you use to measure QC effectiveness?\n\n-   How will you standardize report timeliness between SG6 and SG7?\n\n-   How did you address the direct submission practice by SG82?\n\n-   What is your 30-day corrective-action plan to balance consistency?\n\nExpected Assistant Chief Behaviors (High-Scoring Examples)\n\n-   Conducts audits objectively and constructively.\n\n-   Identifies systemic weaknesses rather than individual blame.\n\n-   Reinforces BC accountability while maintaining trust.\n\n-   Integrates SG82 into suppression documentation channels smoothly.\n\n-   Produces clear, policy-anchored written follow-up.\n\nDeliverables (Candidate)\n\nSubmit within 48 hours of audit:\n\n1.  Battalion QC Compliance Summary Report (2–3 pages).\n\n2.  Corrective Action Tracker with BC assignments & timelines.\n\n3.  Email Summary to Deputy Chief of Operations (CC Training & Fire Chief).\n\nUpload to:\nAbsorb\n\nTraining Objective Reference\n\n-   NFPA 1021 (2020 Ed.) 6.4.2 – Evaluate departmental operations for compliance with policies and procedures.\n\n-   NFPA 1561 & 1500 – Command Accountability and Safety Oversight.\n\n-   SGFD Policy Manual: 104, 205, 301, 302, 410, 900, 902.\n\n-   ISO/PIAL Credit: Administrative Audits and Data Integrity Verification.\n\nOutcome\n\nSuccessful completion demonstrates the Assistant Chief:\n\n-   Can audit Battalion-level QC systems for completeness and consistency.\n\n-   Applies policy and NFPA standards accurately.\n\n-   Improves Battalion Chief accountability through structured feedback.\n\n-   Ensures SG6, SG7, and SG82 are aligned in reporting and documentation standards."
