     "guide": "Module E – Strategic Coordination & Inter-Divisional Operations\n\nPurpose\n\nTo evaluate the Assistant Chief’s ability to plan, coordinate, and maintain operational continuity across multiple divisions (Suppression, Special Ops, Training, EMS, and Administration) during large-scale, high-impact events or extended operations.\nThis module measures foresight, strategic thinking, and executive-level communication.\n\nScenario Overview\n\nScenario Title:\n\n“Unified Command Readiness”\n\nScenario Conditions:\n\nIt’s mid-July, peak hurricane season.\nYou are the Assistant Chief (Shift Commander), responsible for SG6 and SG7 suppression battalions, with coordination oversight for SG82 (Special Operations).\nA slow-moving tropical storm in the Gulf is projected to impact East Baton Rouge Parish within 48 hours.\n\nThe Fire Chief is in a regional planning meeting.\nYou have been directed to lead a 12-hour pre-landfall coordination cycle involving all SGFD divisions.\n\nOver the next 12 hours, you must anticipate and manage the following:\n\n# | Event | Challenge\n1. Resource Forecasting | Two of SG7’s engines are due for scheduled maintenance; Fleet is short on parts. | Determine whether to defer maintenance and notify Admin/Logistics.\n\n2. Training Division Conflict | A multi-company live-fire drill is scheduled for tomorrow. Training wants to proceed; you anticipate storm activation. | Decide whether to cancel, postpone, or modify.\n\n3. Special Ops Readiness | SG82 has requested additional staffing for swift-water prep and boat deployment, which may pull personnel from suppression. | Balance readiness without compromising daily coverage.\n\n4. EMS Integration | EMS Division requests suppression companies to stage medical equipment and assist in potential evacuation shelters. | Coordinate staffing and logistics.\n\n5. Mutual Aid Pre-Planning | BRFD asks for SGFD to pre-position one ladder and one squad for city coverage if impacts are uneven. | Analyze risk vs. internal needs.\n\n6. Public Information Coordination | The PIO drafts a message about sandbag distribution but hasn’t confirmed which stations will host pickup points. | Provide input and confirm with Admin before public release.\n\n7. Personnel Accountability | HR needs a list of essential personnel for possible 48-hour recall. | Verify and submit accurate recall roster for all SG6/SG7 companies.\n\n8. Facility Readiness | Two stations in low-lying areas (61 and 64) lack recent generator tests. | Ensure tests are completed before landfall.\n\nYou are expected to complete a written Strategic Coordination Plan that maintains readiness, communicates across divisions, and supports the Fire Chief’s situational brief.\n\nOfficer Tasks / Decision Points\n\n1.  Develop the Strategic Coordination Plan\n\n- | Forecast operational, logistical, and personnel needs for 48 hours.\n\n- | Prioritize actions and assign responsibilities by division.\n\n2.  Inter-Divisional Communication\n\n- | Convene a coordination meeting (or virtual check-in) with SG6, SG7, SG82, Training, EMS, and Administration.\n\n- | Use ICS terminology (Div/Group/Branch) for clarity.\n\n3.  Operational Continuity\n\n- | Balance suppression readiness with Special Ops prep and Training demands.\n\n- | Document adjustments to apparatus assignments, staffing, and staging.\n\n4.  Information Flow\n\n- | Consolidate status updates for the Fire Chief and Deputy Chiefs.\n\n- | Ensure PIO messaging is accurate and aligned with operational reality.\n\n5.  Documentation & Reporting\n\n- | Submit a Strategic Coordination Summary covering:\n\n- | Forecasted impacts\n\n- | Resource adjustments\n\n- | Division assignments\n\n- | Communication log and timeline\n\nApplicable SGFD Policies\n\nPolicy | Title | Relevance\n104.00 | Chain of Command & Officer Responsibilities | Defines AC authority and coordination expectations.\n\n401.00 | Incident Command General Procedures | Guides multi-division coordination.\n\n410.00 | Mutual Aid & Inter-Division Operations | Governs resource sharing and external coordination.\n\n412.00 | Operational Communications | Sets communication and reporting protocols.\n\n900.00 | Records Management | Documentation standards for strategic operations.\n\nEvaluator Observations\n\nPerformance Element | Max Pts (0–5)\nStrategic Forecasting & Prioritization | 5\n\nInter-Divisional Communication & Coordination | 5\n\nDecision-Making Under Uncertainty | 5\n\nPolicy Application & Documentation Quality | 5\n\nProfessionalism & Executive Presence | 5\n\nTotal (25 pts)\n\nEvaluator Discussion Prompts\n\n-   How did you determine which division’s priorities took precedence?\n\n-   What framework did you use to allocate limited resources?\n\n-   How did you ensure SG82’s readiness without degrading suppression coverage?\n\n-   What communication challenges did you face between divisions?\n\n-   How would you adapt this plan if landfall shifts west and SGFD becomes a host agency for incoming crews?\n\nExpected Assistant Chief Behaviors (High-Scoring Examples)\n\n-   Demonstrates proactive, systems-level thinking.\n\n-   Balances tactical needs with strategic continuity.\n\n-   Communicates clearly across divisions and up the chain.\n\n-   Maintains composure and flexibility under dynamic conditions.\n\n-   Produces professional documentation usable at the EOC or executive briefing level.\n\nDeliverables (Candidate)\n\nSubmit within 24 hours:\n\n1.  Strategic Coordination Summary Report (3–5 pages).\n\n2.  Division Action Matrix (task, responsible officer, deadline).\n\n3.  Email Summary to Fire Chief & Deputy Chiefs (CC Training, EMS, and Admin).\n\nUpload to:\nAbsorb\n\nTraining Objective Reference\n\n-   NFPA 1021 (2020 Ed.) 6.4.7–6.5.4 – Manage multi-agency operations, coordinate division resources, and maintain continuity of command.\n\n-   SGFD Policy Manual: 104, 401, 410, 412, 900.\n\n-   ISO / PIAL Credit: Inter-Divisional Planning and Continuity Documentation.\n\nOutcome\n\nSuccessful completion demonstrates that the Assistant Chief:\n\n-   Anticipates and manages strategic, cross-divisional demands under pressure.\n\n-   Maintains readiness across Suppression (SG6 & SG7), Special Ops (SG82), Training, and EMS.\n\n-   Communicates clearly with executive leadership and external partners.\n\n-   Produces actionable, policy-compliant documentation suitable for command-staff briefings.\n\n-   Exhibits readiness for Deputy Chief-level strategic command responsibility."
